## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft DP with a left-anchored subarray value and exact block counts, plus reconstruction for endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exactly k blocks
- Precomputation of bestStartAt[l][i]
- Reconstructing block boundaries via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeBestStartDP(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const bestStart = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(-Infinity));
  for (let l=0; l<n; l++) {
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let i=l; i<n; i++) {
      const sum = pref[i+1]-pref[l];
      best = Math.max(best, sum);
      bestStart[l][i] = best;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = bestStart[0][i];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=t-1; i<n; i++) {
      let bestVal = -Infinity, bestL = -1;
      for (let l=t-1; l<=i; l++) {
        const prev = (l-1>=0) ? dp[l-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        const val = prev + bestStart[l][i];
        if (val > bestVal) { bestVal = val; bestL = l; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = bestVal;
      par[i][t] = bestL;
